WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:04.580
When you're working with an actor as talented
as Joaquin Phoenix, you learn to expect and

00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:06.460
welcome the unexpected.

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:10.100
“Haha.”

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:14.680
In a wide-ranging interview with Slashfilm,
Joker cinematographer Lawrence Sher offered

00:00:14.690 --> 00:00:17.779
some insights into one of the film's most
intriguing scenes.

00:00:17.780 --> 00:00:19.390
Basically, no one knew it was coming.

00:00:19.540 --> 00:00:23.360
We're going to be discussing a few important
plot points here, so consider yourself warned:

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:27.220
if you haven't seen Joker yet, there's going
to be a handful of potential spoilers ahead.

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:31.360
During the scene in question, Arthur Fleck
is clearly struggling in his own mind as he

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:33.540
rattles around his kitchen, unable to sleep.

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:37.800
He begins obsessively cleaning out the contents
of his refrigerator, and anyone who's ever

00:00:37.809 --> 00:00:42.100
suffered a bout of insomnia will undoubtedly
feel for him up to a point, anyway.

00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:46.710
Later on in the scene, something entirely
odd happens: Once the fridge is emptied out,

00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:51.080
Arthur crawls inside, shuts the door behind
him, and... well, he just stays in there.

00:00:51.289 --> 00:00:55.440
It's a unique and entirely brilliant way to
shine a light on the inner workings of Arthur's

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:56.520
tortured soul.

00:00:56.820 --> 00:01:00.410
And Phoenix reportedly totally improvised
this highly memorable moment.

00:01:00.410 --> 00:01:01.360
As Sher tells Slashfilm,

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:05.140
"When he climbed in the refrigerator, we had
no idea he was going to do that.

00:01:05.380 --> 00:01:09.220
We set up two camera positions, and Joaquin
just thought about what he would do if he

00:01:09.220 --> 00:01:10.460
was a massive insomniac.

00:01:10.460 --> 00:01:14.251
Again, we lit it so he could go anywhere,
and the first and only time he did it, we

00:01:14.251 --> 00:01:15.251
were mesmerized.

00:01:15.251 --> 00:01:17.110
I remember thinking, 'What is he doing?

00:01:17.110 --> 00:01:18.720
Did he just crawl in the fridge?'"

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:22.120
The answer to that question is: Yes, he most
certainly did.

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:26.900
And of course, the shot was so utterly bizarre
it remained in the final cut of the film.

00:01:26.910 --> 00:01:30.710
Phoenix was given a massive amount of creative
freedom as he developed his interpretation

00:01:30.710 --> 00:01:34.950
of the character … and that creative freedom
proved to be the key to Joker's success.

00:01:34.950 --> 00:01:39.130
Sher says his biggest fear while shooting
was inadvertently interfering with the actor's

00:01:39.130 --> 00:01:39.620
process.

00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:43.980
In fact, during the early days of Joker's
shoot, Sher confessed he was rather intimidated

00:01:43.980 --> 00:01:46.180
by Phoenix… and really, who wouldn't be?

00:01:46.420 --> 00:01:50.160
“Haha.”

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:53.180
Sher says he did his best not to step on the
actor's toes:

00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:57.080
"In the beginning, I didn't really talk to
him that much, but he actually broke the ice

00:01:57.080 --> 00:01:57.580
first.

00:01:57.700 --> 00:02:01.660
He apologized, saying, 'Sorry, I know I'm
acting a little odd, but I'm just trying to

00:02:01.660 --> 00:02:02.660
do this thing.'

00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:05.960
I went, [...] 'Please keep doing whatever
you’re doing because it’s fantastic.

00:02:05.970 --> 00:02:08.940
If you never talk to me during the rest of
this movie, I’m 100% cool with that, too.'

00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:10.980
I didn’t want to get in the way."

00:02:11.260 --> 00:02:14.180
But their working relationship changed significantly
during the shoot.

00:02:14.180 --> 00:02:15.180
Sher reveals that,

00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:19.280
"Once we started shooting scenes with him
as Joker, Joker's confidence meant Joaquin

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:21.080
was opening up more to me, off screen.

00:02:21.320 --> 00:02:25.319
Joaquin started watching the dailies to see
what I was doing and we started talking more.

00:02:25.319 --> 00:02:28.909
By the end of the movie, it was a much more
open conversation, in ways we could serve

00:02:28.909 --> 00:02:29.400
each other."

00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:34.260
A surprising amount of Phoenix's improvisations
wound up making it to the screen and many

00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:37.700
of those spontaneous moments involved intensely
disturbing dancing.

00:02:37.930 --> 00:02:41.800
For instance, the sequence in the bathroom
was only scripted up to a point: Phoenix knew

00:02:41.819 --> 00:02:45.989
that Arthur had to hide his gun and wash off
his makeup but the rest was very much left

00:02:45.989 --> 00:02:46.880
up to the actor.

00:02:47.129 --> 00:02:50.980
Director Todd Phillips previously revealed
that the scene that features Arthur dancing

00:02:50.980 --> 00:02:54.909
and strutting down a long stone staircase
wasn't originally in the script, either.

00:02:54.909 --> 00:02:59.099
The memorable moment came about as Phoenix
found new ways to demonstrate the evolution

00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:00.820
or devolution of his character.

00:03:01.140 --> 00:03:02.139
As Sher explains:

00:03:02.139 --> 00:03:06.030
"We just [...] let it happen, which became
a major part of how we did a lot of things."

00:03:06.030 --> 00:03:08.320
Well, it was clearly a winning strategy.

00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:12.020
As Entertainment Weekly reported on October
25th, 2019:

00:03:12.020 --> 00:03:15.300
"Joker is now the highest-grossing R-rated
film of all time."

00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:19.160
It seems like great things happen when you
stand back and let Joaquin Phoenix work his

00:03:19.160 --> 00:03:21.420
magic… his creepy, creepy magic.

00:03:21.660 --> 00:03:23.340
“Haha.”

